Sure, here's a sample implementation in Java using the JWNL package:
```java
import net.sf.extjwnl.data.IndexWord;
import net.sf.extjwnl.data.POS;
import net.sf.extjwnl.data.Synset;
import net.sf.extjwnl.data.Word;
import net.sf.extjwnl.dictionary.Dictionary;
public class SynonymFinder {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // initialize the JWNL dictionary
        String path = "path/to/your/wordnet/dict";
        Dictionary dict = Dictionary.getDefaultResourceInstance(path);

        // get the IndexWord for 'assistance'
        String word = "assistance";
        POS pos = POS.VERB; // assuming 'assistance' is used as a verb in the context
        IndexWord indexWord = dict.getIndexWord(pos, word);

        // get all synonyms of the IndexWord
        Synset[] synsets = indexWord.getSenses();
        for (Synset synset : synsets) {
            for (Word synonym : synset.getWords()) {
                // compute the similarity score between the synonym and 'assistance'
                double similarity = synset.getGloss().toLowerCase().split(word).length - 1;

                // print the synonym and its similarity score
                System.out.println(synonym.getLemma() + " (" + similarity + ")");
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we first initialize the JWNL dictionary with the path to your WordNet installation. Then, we get the IndexWord for the word 'assistance' and its part of speech (verb in this case). We then iterate through all the synonyms of the IndexWord, computing the similarity score between each synonym and 'assistance'. The similarity score is simply the number of times the word 'assistance' appears in the gloss of the synonym's Synset. Finally, we print out each synonym and its similarity score. The synonym with the highest similarity score is the best match for 'assistance' in the given context.

