To modify the C# code to sort by saturation level before applying the filters, you can use the following steps:
1. Create a new class that implements the `IComparer<Color>` interface. This class will be used to compare two colors based on their saturation levels.
```
public class SaturationComparer : IComparer<Color>
{
    public int Compare(Color x, Color y)
    {
        float xSaturation = x.GetSaturation();
        float ySaturation = y.GetSaturation();
        if (xSaturation < ySaturation)
        {
            return -1;
        }
        else if (xSaturation > ySaturation)
        {
            return 1;
        }
        else
        {
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```
2. Modify the `Main` method to create an instance of the `SaturationComparer` class and use it to sort the colors by saturation level before applying the filters.
```
static void Main(string[] args)
{
    List<Color> colors = new List<Color>();
    colors.Add(Color.FromArgb(255, 255, 255)); // white
    colors.Add(Color.FromArgb(0, 0, 0)); // black
    colors.Add(Color.FromArgb(255, 0, 0)); // red
    colors.Add(Color.FromArgb(0, 255, 0)); // green
    colors.Add(Color.FromArgb(0, 0, 255)); // blue
    colors.Sort(new SaturationComparer());
    colors = colors.Where(c => c.GetSaturation() >= 0.5f && c.GetBrightness() >= 0.7f).ToList();
    foreach (Color color in colors)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(color.Name);
    }
    Console.ReadLine();
}
```
With these modifications, the code will now sort the colors by saturation level before applying the filters.

