To ensure secure authentication using JSON Web Tokens (JWTs), you can follow these best practices:
1. Use strong secret keys to sign and verify JWTs.
2. Set appropriate expiration times to limit the lifespan of JWTs.
3. Use HTTPS to transmit JWTs securely over the network.
4. Avoid storing sensitive information in JWTs.
Here's an example of how to implement JWT-based authentication in an HTML page using JavaScript:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>JWT Authentication Example</title>
	<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jsrsasign/8.0.12/jsrsasign-all-min.js"></script>
	<script>
		// Function to generate JWT
		function generateJWT() {
			// Set header
			var header = { "alg": "HS256", "typ": "JWT" };

			// Set payload
			var payload = { "sub": "1234567890", "name": "John Doe", "iat": 1516239022 };

			// Set secret key
			var secretKey = "mysecretkey";

			// Sign JWT
			var jwt = KJUR.jws.JWS.sign("HS256", JSON.stringify(header), JSON.stringify(payload), secretKey);

			// Store JWT in local storage
			localStorage.setItem("jwt", jwt);
		}

		// Function to verify JWT
		function verifyJWT() {
			// Get JWT from local storage
			var jwt = localStorage.getItem("jwt");

			// Set secret key
			var secretKey = "mysecretkey";

			// Verify JWT
			var isValid = KJUR.jws.JWS.verify(jwt, secretKey, ["HS256"]);

			// If JWT is valid, redirect to dashboard
			if (isValid) {
				window.location.href = "dashboard.html";
			}
		}
	</script>
</head>
<body>
	<button onclick="generateJWT()">Generate JWT</button>
	<button onclick="verifyJWT()">Verify JWT</button>
</body>
</html>
```
In this example, we use the jsrsasign library to generate and verify JWTs. The `generateJWT()` function generates a JWT with a header, payload, and secret key, and stores it in local storage. The `verifyJWT()` function retrieves the JWT from local storage, verifies it using the secret key, and redirects the user to the dashboard if the JWT is valid.

