Certainly! Here's an implementation of Selection Sort in C++ using the `vector` data type:
```c++
#include <vector>
void selectionSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
                minIndex = j;
            }
        }
        if (minIndex != i) {
            std::swap(arr[i], arr[minIndex]);
        }
    }
}
```
The Selection Sort algorithm works by repeatedly selecting the smallest element from the unsorted portion of the array and swapping it with the element at the beginning of the unsorted portion. The algorithm maintains two subarrays within the main array: the sorted subarray and the unsorted subarray. Initially, the sorted subarray is empty and the unsorted subarray is the entire array.
The algorithm then iterates through the unsorted subarray and finds the smallest element. This element is then swapped with the first element of the unsorted subarray, effectively adding it to the sorted subarray. The algorithm then repeats this process with the remaining unsorted subarray until the entire array is sorted.
The time complexity of Selection Sort is O(n^2), where n is the size of the array. This is because the algorithm must iterate through the unsorted subarray n times, and for each iteration it must find the smallest element in the unsorted subarray, which takes O(n) time. Therefore, the total time complexity is O(n^2).
In terms of performance and efficiency, Selection Sort is not the most efficient sorting algorithm. Its time complexity of O(n^2) makes it impractical for large input sizes. However, it does have the advantage of being simple to implement and requiring only O(1) additional space. It also has the property of being a stable sort, meaning that it preserves the relative order of equal elements in the array.
Other sorting algorithms, such as Quicksort and Merge Sort, have better time complexity than Selection Sort, with O(nlogn) and O(nlogn) respectively. However, they also require additional space for their implementation, making them less space-efficient than Selection Sort. The choice of sorting algorithm ultimately depends on the specific requirements of the problem at hand, such as the size of the input and the desired time and space complexity.

To display a histogram of sound frequency distribution from an audio file using HTML, you can use the Web Audio API and the D3.js library. Here's an example HTML code:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Sound Frequency Histogram</title>
  <script src="https://d3js.org/d3.v5.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <input type="file" id="fileInput">
  <canvas id="canvas"></canvas>
  <script>
    const canvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    const fileInput = document.getElementById('fileInput');
    fileInput.addEventListener('change', function(event) {
      const file = event.target.files[0];
      const reader = new FileReader();
      reader.onload = function(event) {
        const arrayBuffer = event.target.result;
        const audioContext = new AudioContext();
        audioContext.decodeAudioData(arrayBuffer, function(audioBuffer) {
          const frequencyData = getFrequencyData(audioBuffer);
          plotHistogram(frequencyData);
        });
      };
      reader.readAsArrayBuffer(file);
    });
    function getFrequencyData(audioBuffer) {
      const audioContext = new AudioContext();
      const source = audioContext.createBufferSource();
      source.buffer = audioBuffer;
      const analyser = audioContext.createAnalyser();
      analyser.fftSize = 2048;
      source.connect(analyser);
      analyser.connect(audioContext.destination);
      const bufferLength = analyser.frequencyBinCount;
      const frequencyData = new Uint8Array(bufferLength);
      analyser.getByteFrequencyData(frequencyData);
      return frequencyData;
    }
    function plotHistogram(frequencyData) {
      const xScale = d3.scaleLog()
        .domain([20, 20000])
        .range([0, canvas.width]);
      const yScale = d3.scaleLog()
        .domain([1, 256])
        .range([canvas.height, 0]);
      const histogram = d3.histogram()
        .value(function(d) { return d; })
        .domain(xScale.domain())
        .thresholds(xScale.ticks());
      const bins = histogram(frequencyData);
      ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
      ctx.fillStyle = 'steelblue';
      bins.forEach(function(bin) {
        ctx.fillRect(
          xScale(bin.x0),
          yScale(bin.length),
          xScale(bin.x1) - xScale(bin.x0),
          yScale(1) - yScale(bin.length)
        );
      });
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```
This code creates an input element for selecting an audio file and a canvas element for displaying the histogram. When a file is selected, the code reads its contents as an array buffer and decodes it using the Web Audio API. Then, it gets the frequency data of the audio using a Fourier transform and plots a histogram using D3.js. The x-axis is scaled logarithmically from 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz, and the y-axis is also scaled logarithmically from 1 to 256, which is the maximum value of the frequency data. The histogram is plotted using rectangles with heights proportional to the frequency counts and widths proportional to the frequency ranges.

