Here's the C++ code implementation for finding the largest number in an array of integers using binary search algorithm:
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int findLargest(int arr[], int n) {
    int low = 0, high = n - 1;
    while (low < high) {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] < arr[high]) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid;
        }
    }
    return arr[high];
}
int main() {
    int arr[] = {5, 8, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    int largest = findLargest(arr, n);
    cout << "The largest number in the array is " << largest << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this implementation, we first initialize the `low` and `high` variables as the first and last indices of the array, respectively. We then use a `while` loop to repeatedly divide the array in half until we find the largest number. In each iteration, we calculate the `mid` index as the average of `low` and `high`. If the value at `mid` is less than the value at `high`, then we know that the largest number is in the second half of the array, so we update `low` to be `mid + 1`. Otherwise, we know that the largest number is in the first half of the array, so we update `high` to be `mid`. We continue this process until `low` and `high` are equal, at which point we know that we have found the largest number in the array, which is located at the `high` index.

