The binary search algorithm is a method for finding a specific value in a sorted array of values. It works by repeatedly dividing in half the portion of the array that could contain the desired value, until the value is found or it is determined that the value is not in the array.
Here is a Java code implementation that finds the desired element in a sorted array, and also counts the number of times the search function is called before finding the element, without using any loop constructs:
```
public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target, int low, int high, int count) {
    if (low > high) {
        return -1;
    }

    int mid = (low + high) / 2;

    if (arr[mid] == target) {
        return count + 1;
    } else if (arr[mid] > target) {
        return binarySearch(arr, target, low, mid - 1, count + 1);
    } else {
        return binarySearch(arr, target, mid + 1, high, count + 1);
    }
}
// Example usage:
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9};
int target = 5;
int count = 0;
int result = binarySearch(arr, target, 0, arr.length - 1, count);
if (result == -1) {
    System.out.println("Element not found");
} else {
    System.out.println("Element found at index " + result);
}
```
The `binarySearch` function takes in an array `arr`, a target value `target`, a lower index `low`, an upper index `high`, and a count of the number of times the function has been called so far `count`. The function recursively calls itself, dividing the array in half each time until the target value is found or it is determined that the value is not in the array. If the target value is found, the function returns the count of the number of times it has been called plus one. If the target value is not found, the function returns -1.
Note that this implementation uses recursion instead of loops to achieve the desired result.

