To calculate the frequency of a guitar string, you can use the following formula:
f = (1/2L) * sqrt(T/m)
where:
- f is the frequency of the string in Hertz (Hz)
- L is the length of the string in meters (m)
- T is the tension of the string in Newtons (N)
- m is the mass per unit length of the string in kilograms per meter (kg/m)
Here is a sample Java code that calculates the frequency of a guitar string based on its tension, length, and thickness:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class GuitarStringFrequency {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the tension of the guitar string in Newtons: ");
        double tension = input.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the length of the guitar string in meters: ");
        double length = input.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the thickness of the guitar string in meters: ");
        double thickness = input.nextDouble();
        double mass = thickness * 0.00785; // assuming steel string
        double frequency = (1 / (2 * length)) * Math.sqrt(tension / mass);
        System.out.println("The frequency of the guitar string is " + frequency + " Hz.");
    }
}
```
In this code, we first prompt the user to input the tension, length, and thickness of the guitar string. We then calculate the mass of the string based on its thickness (assuming a steel string), and finally use the formula to calculate the frequency of the string. The result is then printed to the console.

