In JavaScript, the keyword 'this' refers to the object that the function is a property of. When used in regular functions, 'this' is determined by the context in which the function is called. However, when used in arrow functions, 'this' is determined by the context in which the arrow function is defined.
Here is an example of a regular function:
```
const person = {
  name: 'John',
  sayName: function() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
person.sayName(); // Output: John
```
In this example, 'this' refers to the 'person' object, which is the object that the 'sayName' function is a property of. 
Here is an example of an arrow function:
```
const person = {
  name: 'John',
  sayName: () => {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
person.sayName(); // Output: undefined
```
In this example, 'this' refers to the global object, which is different from the 'person' object. This is because arrow functions do not have their own 'this' value, so they inherit it from the surrounding context.
When it comes to object methods versus constructor functions, the value of 'this' can be different. In object methods, 'this' refers to the object that the method is a property of. In constructor functions, 'this' refers to the instance of the object that is being created.
Here is an example of an object method:
```
const person = {
  name: 'John',
  sayName: function() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
person.sayName(); // Output: John
```
Here is an example of a constructor function:
```
function Person(name) {
  this.name = name;
  this.sayName = function() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
const john = new Person('John');
john.sayName(); // Output: John
```
To manipulate the value of 'this', the 'bind', 'call', and 'apply' methods can be used. These methods allow you to set the value of 'this' explicitly.
The 'bind' method creates a new function with the same body as the original function, but with a specified 'this' value:
```
const person = {
  name: 'John',
  sayName: function() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
const sayName = person.sayName.bind({ name: 'Jane' });
sayName(); // Output: Jane
```
The 'call' method calls a function with a specified 'this' value and arguments:
```
const person = {
  name: 'John',
  sayName: function() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
person.sayName.call({ name: 'Jane' }); // Output: Jane
```
The 'apply' method calls a function with a specified 'this' value and an array of arguments:
```
const person = {
  name: 'John',
  sayName: function() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}
person.sayName.apply({ name: 'Jane' }); // Output: Jane
```
In Python, the equivalent of 'this' is 'self'. In C++, the equivalent of 'this' is a pointer to the current object. However, the behavior of 'this' in JavaScript is different from these languages because of the way it is determined.

